// r24.13 (review A§10): the two sentences that stood here were wrong in both
// directions and a later reader would have used them to decide a placement was
// still safe. It was "two of the three" (it is all three, since
// ATHENA_ACUITY_PIVOT_ANNOUNCE), and it claimed this placement was
// "announce-safe at all five call sites at once" (it was not safe at ANY of the
// announcing ones — see review A§1: the announce's `.done` wait was a bounded
// guess, so the drain could be entered with her voice still in the air). What
// is true now: the fifth site, the album-recall drain, has always been safe
// because it announces through stream_tts_oneshot → stream_tts_wait_done, and
// the other four are safe because the announce is now waited for with the SAME
// proof (stream_tts_done_proven) and aseam::look_tape_due refuses the window
// while nothing proved she had finished. Placement
// inside vision_drain_eval is still the right one — it is where the budget
// being waited on actually lives — but it is the PROOF, not the placement, that
// keeps her voice out of his tape.
// r24.13 (review D§4): that proof is now CARRIED, not looked up. It was a
// file-scope flag written only by stream_tts_done_proven, so on the two drains
// that never announce it was the leftover of an unrelated utterance and one
// timed-out chime could latch the tape shut for the session with nothing
// printed. It is a parameter of this window and of vision_drain_eval now,
// stated at every call site the way live_turn is, and composed by
// aseam::her_audio_settled. The lambda PRINTS an interesting refusal, so a shut
// tape is never silent again.
// It is a hook, not a mechanism: the recorder
// is acmp::OutageTape, unchanged, and main() supplies the arm/close it already
// uses for compaction. Unset (the no-vision build, or before main installs it)
// the guard is inert.
//
// It returns whether THIS window owns the tape, and the whole arming rule lives
// in aseam::look_tape_due — one spelling, so the fixture pins production's rule
// rather than a paraphrase of it. A window that does not own the tape must not
// close one: a compaction tape already rolling covers this wait on its own and
// its own consumer takes it, and stopping it here would join a thread another
// path is draining.
// ATHENA_VISION_LOOK_TAPE=0 (or ATHENA_OUTAGE_TAPE_SEAM=0, or ATHENA_OUTAGE_MIC=0)
// → look_tape_due is false, nothing is armed, started or stopped, and the drain
// is r24.12 exactly, including its audio.clear().
[[maybe_unused]] static std::function<bool(bool, int, bool)> g_look_tape_window;
struct LookTapeWindow {
    int  wait_ms_ = 0;
    bool settled_ = false;   // r24.13 (review D§4): this drain's own answer
    bool own_     = false;
    // NO DEFAULT ARGUMENT on `her_audio_settled`, for the reason
    // aseam::look_tape_due states at its fifth term: a call-site assumption is
    // what produced the defect, so every construction says which it is.
    LookTapeWindow(int wait_ms, bool her_audio_settled)
        : wait_ms_(wait_ms), settled_(her_audio_settled) {
        if (g_look_tape_window) own_ = g_look_tape_window(true, wait_ms_, settled_);
    }
    // Every return path in the drain — DEFER, CLOSE_DEFER_CAP, FAIL, the
    // success tail, the sync path's own failures — closes the window, because
    // the deaf window ends when the function does. r24.12's two unclosed
    // audio.clear() paths are the reason this is an RAII guard and not two
    // statements a later edit could get between.
    // ── r24.13 (review A§6): a destructor is implicitly noexcept ────────────
    // What was wrong: this line called an ALLOCATING consumer through a
    // std::function it did not re-check, from a noexcept context — so either
    // throw was std::terminate, not an error. The close reaches
    // outage_tape_settle → acmp::OutageTape::trim_to_speech, which builds
    // `std::vector<float> keep(...)` out of up to a four-minute tape (~15 MB);
    // std::bad_alloc there is not hypothetical on a 7 GB box running the
    // sanitizer lanes. And the constructor tests g_look_tape_window while this
    // did not — today nothing clears the hook between the two, but the guard's
    // own reason for existing is that "a later edit could not get between", and
    // an asymmetric null check is exactly what a later edit trips over
    // (std::bad_function_call → the same std::terminate). SIGABRT here would
    // fire the launcher's ERR trap and the session's cross-session memory would
    // never be flushed, against [brain-resilience]'s _exit(0) discipline.
    // own_ is cleared FIRST so a re-entrant path cannot double-close.
    ~LookTapeWindow() {
        if (!own_) return;
        own_ = false;
        if (!g_look_tape_window) return;
        try { (void) g_look_tape_window(false, wait_ms_, settled_); } catch (...) { }
    }
    LookTapeWindow(const LookTapeWindow &) = delete;
    LookTapeWindow &operator=(const LookTapeWindow &) = delete;
};
