                                // r24.6 (WO-52 / S19 G7): 4 → 3 on the first
                                // flush. find_sentence_end(text, 4) could not
                                // flush a 3-char opener, so "Oh." (the space
                                // sits at index 3, and 3 >= 4 is false) waited
                                // for the NEXT sentence before anything was
                                // sent — dead air at the one moment he is most
                                // sensitive to it. A short first chunk is
                                // allowed; the famine it can open is what the
                                // WO-49 target-fill guard now bridges.
                                size_t floor = streaming_started ? 15 : 3;   // first flush of a turn fires early
                                size_t split = find_sentence_end(pending_speech, floor);

                                // First-flush fallback: a long opening sentence with no
                                // sentence-end (.?!) yet would otherwise block all audio
                                // until the whole sentence is generated. Once it grows past
                                // a soft cap, break at the last clause boundary so chunk 1
                                // stays short and first speech arrives sooner. Only applies
                                // before the first flush of a turn; afterwards the normal
                                // sentence-boundary logic governs.
                                // r19.6 (C61): fire on the EXISTENCE of a clause
                                // boundary, not on total length.
                                //
                                // The fallback only ran once `pending_speech`
                                // passed 80 bytes — roughly 20 tokens, about
                                // three seconds of generation — and then took
                                // the LAST boundary, which contradicts its own
                                // stated intent ("so chunk 1 stays short and
                                // first speech arrives sooner"). On the very
                                // common opening clause with no terminal
                                // punctuation, the comma that made the line
                                // speakable was typically available a second and
                                // a half before anything was sent. That is dead
                                // air at the single moment the user is most
                                // sensitive to it: right after he stopped
                                // talking.
                                //
